families.bracket-fixed-join.formal:
For the composite of two conformal spinors (twistors) of mass $m$ at $\Lambda > 0$, the exact join of Fact \claimref{families.imported-dictionary-exact-join} fixes the deformed bracket:
\begin{equation}
|B_{\Lambda}|^{2} \;=\; \frac{m^{2}}{2} - \frac{\Lambda}{6}\,C \;=\; \frac{\Lambda}{6}\Bigl(\nu^{2} + \frac{9}{4} - C\Bigr),
\end{equation}
the second form for a composite on the principal series, $w = -\tfrac32 \pm I_4\nu$ (numbers are read in $\mathrm{span}\{1, I_4\}$). Consequently $B_{\Lambda} \ne 0$ if and only if $m^{2} > \Lambda C/3$, which for $C$ of order one is every composite heavier than the Hubble scale; the flat bracket $\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}$ is then nonzero up to the $\Lambda$-suppressed $\omega$-area of the join. The identity is a rewriting of the join and says nothing about a vacuum: it converts the matter programme's open equation into the equation the broken phase must satisfy, at consistency level, without supplying the potential that selects the phase, the $W$ and $Z$ masses, or the weak mixing angle. At observation level, and flagged as such: read with Fact \claimref{families.imported-face-valued-bracket-quantum-numbers}, the nonvanishing bracket is a charged singlet with a fixed modulus, the shape of a condensed order parameter, with the reservation recorded in that Fact about what it is an order parameter \emph{of}.

__notes__.note.mass.setting-grip-composite-frozen-:
For two twistors, write $t_{il} := \hp{\pi^{i}}{\omega^{l}}$ ($i, l \in \{1,2\}$) for the $2 \times 2$ matrix of mixed pairings, and
\begin{equation}
h_{il} := \bar{Z}^{i}\cdot Z^{l} = t_{il} + \overline{t_{li}}, \qquad
C := \tfrac14\,(h_{11} - h_{22})^{2} + |h_{12}|^{2} = \tfrac12\tr\bigl(h_{0}^{2}\bigr) = \tfrac14\bigl[(\tr h)^{2} - 4\det h\bigr],
\end{equation}
for the Hermitian Gram matrix $h$ of the composite and its traceless part $h_{0}$. The diagonal entries $h_{ii} = \bar{Z}^{i}\cdot Z^{i}$ are twice the constituents' helicities; $C$ is the quadratic Casimir of the internal $\mathfrak{su}(2)$ that mixes the two constituents, a quarter of the squared difference of the eigenvalues of $h$, nonnegative, and zero exactly when $h$ is a multiple of the identity.

__notes__.note.mass.what-nullity-fixes:
With the undetermined slot coefficient $X_{c}(x) = n_{0} + x + c\,Q(x)\,n_{\infty}$ one has $Q(X_{c}) = Q(x)(1 + 2c)$, so nullity of the point embedding for all $x$ fixes $c = -\tfrac12$. This is a statement about the coefficient, not about the chart's normalisation: rescaling the volume form $\varepsilon$ by $\kappa$ rescales $\ip{n_{0}}{n_{\infty}}$, $Q(x)$ and $Q(X_{c})$ alike, and nullity is insensitive to $\kappa$. What fixes the chart is the imported normalisation $\ip{n_{0}}{n_{\infty}} = +1$ together with $Q = Q(x)$ (Fact \claimref{mass.imported-flat-model-twistor-chart-infinity}); on the $x$-slot this reads $\ip{x}{y} = -\varepsilon_{ab}\varepsilon_{a'b'}x^{aa'}y^{bb'}$, so $Q(x) = -2\det(x^{aa'})$. The real points of the $x$-slot are, by definition, the $x$ whose translation generator $A(x\wedge n_{\infty})$ lies in $\mathfrak{su}(2,2)$ for the Hermitian form the relation displayed with \claimref{mass.chart-form-spin-isomorphism} fixed below (the reversion form $h$ on the conformal spinor space $\CS$, written in the chart's frame); by Lemma \claimref{mass.chart-form-spin-isomorphism}(2) that generator's block is $b_{x} = x\varepsilon$, and $x\varepsilon$ is anti-Hermitian iff $x = \tfrac{i}{\sqrt2}K\varepsilon$ with $K$ Hermitian (if $x = \tfrac{i}{\sqrt2}K\varepsilon$ then $x\varepsilon = -\tfrac{i}{\sqrt2}K$; conversely $K := i\sqrt2\,x\varepsilon$ is Hermitian when $x\varepsilon$ is anti-Hermitian, and $x = -\tfrac{i}{\sqrt2}K\varepsilon^{-1} = \tfrac{i}{\sqrt2}K\varepsilon$). So the real points are the matrices
\begin{equation}
x^{aa'} = \frac{i}{\sqrt2}\,K\varepsilon, \qquad K = \begin{pmatrix} x^{0}+x^{3} & x^{1}-ix^{2} \\ x^{1}+ix^{2} & x^{0}-x^{3}\end{pmatrix},\quad \varepsilon = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ -1 & 0\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation}
with $(x^{0}, x^{1}, x^{2}, x^{3})$ real, on which $Q = -2\det x = \det K = (x^{0})^{2} - (x^{1})^{2} - (x^{2})^{2} - (x^{3})^{2}$; the factor $i$ is that of the incidence relation (in geometric algebra the conformal pseudoscalar $I_6$, the complex unit of $\CS$, so that $x^{aa'}$ is $H_x$ up to that unit and the normalisation), and the factor $\varepsilon$ is what makes the translation generators of Lemma \claimref{mass.chart-form-spin-isomorphism} anti-Hermitian for the relation displayed with \claimref{mass.chart-form-spin-isomorphism} (Lemma \claimref{mass.grip-real}). The correspondence paper's reality structure \cite[Prop.~4.5]{ButlerT9} selects the same slice up to a choice of frame in $\Sc'$, which changes no formula of this section: the computations below are polynomial identities in the chart. The script checks these statements with the slice scaled by $\sqrt2$ (so that $Q = 2\det K$ there), the normalisation $1/\sqrt2$ being a real scalar that affects no reality statement; it also confirms that the anti-Hermiticity condition on the eight real parameters of $x$ has rank four, so that the slice is all of the real locus.

__notes__.note.mass.setting-grip-composite-frozen-:
The classical phase space of $N$ twistors carries the Hermitian form $\Sigma$ of signature $(2,2)$, written $\bar{Z}\cdot W := \Sigma(Z, W)$, and the conformal group acts by $SU(2,2)$. The form is canonical up to a phase and has signature $(2,2)$ \cite[Prop.~4.5(1), (2), (4)]{ButlerT9}; the chart frame fixes the phase, and in it $\Sigma$ is the standard twistor form $\bar{Z}\cdot Z = \omega^{A}\bar\pi_{A} + \bar\omega^{A'}\pi_{A'}$ \cite{PenroseRindler1986}, which pairs $\omega$ with $\bar\pi$ through the frame: for $Z = (\omega, \pi)$ and $W = (\xi, \chi)$,
\begin{equation}
\bar{Z}\cdot W = \hp{\pi}{\xi} + \overline{\hp{\chi}{\omega}}, \qquad \hp{u}{v} := \overline{u^{3}}\,v^{1} + \overline{u^{4}}\,v^{2}\quad (u \in \Sc',\ v \in \Sc),
\end{equation}
the sesquilinear pairing that identifies the frame $e_{3}, e_{4}$ of $\Sc'$ with the frame $e_{1}, e_{2}$ of $\Sc$; we write $\bar{u}_{a}$ for the conjugate of the component of $u \in \Sc'$ so identified with $e_{a}$, so that $\hp{u}{v} = \bar{u}_{a}v^{a}$. The real slice \claimref{note.mass.what-nullity-fixes} is the one this form determines. The moment map of the action on the phase space is the classical form of the generators of Fact \claimref{mass.imported-flat-model-twistor-chart-infinity}:
\begin{equation}
M_{A}(Z^{1}, \dots, Z^{N}) := I_{6}\sum_{i=1}^{N} \bar{Z}^{i}\cdot(A Z^{i}), \qquad A \in \mathfrak{sl}(\T),
\end{equation}
with $I_{6}$ the complex unit of the conformal spinor space (acting on conformal spinors by right multiplication; values of $\Sigma$ and components are read in $\mathrm{span}\{1, I_{6}\}$, the bar reversing $I_{6}$), real for $A \in \mathfrak{su}(2,2)$, and $M_{U\wedge V} := M_{A(U\wedge V)}$ for ambient $U, V$. Equivariance is built in: for $\gamma \in SU(2,2)$, $M_{A}(\gamma Z) = M_{\gamma^{-1}A\gamma}(Z)$, and $A(\gamma U \wedge \gamma V) = \gamma\,A(U\wedge V)\,\gamma^{-1}$ because the isomorphism of Fact \claimref{mass.imported-flat-model-twistor-chart-infinity} intertwines the two actions.

mass.casimir-form-join.formal:
Fix a composite $Z^{1}, \dots, Z^{N}$ of conformal spinors with Hermitian Gram matrix $h_{il} = \bar{Z}^{i}\cdot Z^{l}$. For a nondegenerate subspace $W$ of the ambient space with basis $(e_{\alpha})$ and inverse Gram matrix $(g^{\alpha\beta})$, put $M_{\alpha\beta} := M_{e_{\alpha}\wedge e_{\beta}}$ and $C_{2}(W) := \tfrac12\, g^{\alpha\alpha'}g^{\beta\beta'}M_{\alpha\beta}M_{\alpha'\beta'}$, the quadratic Casimir of the rotations of $W$ evaluated on the composite; $C_{2}(\Lambda^{2}\CS)$ is the conformal Casimir $C_{2}(\mathfrak{so}(2,4))$ of the composite, and for the frozen point $C_{2}(I_{\Lambda}^{\perp})$ is its de Sitter Casimir $C_{2}(\mathfrak{so}(1,4))$, both as quadratic forms on the moment components; in this normalisation $C_{2}(I_{\Lambda}^{\perp})$ is the de Sitter Casimir of the scalar-module convention of Fact \claimref{mass.imported-unitary-representations-de-sitter-group} (acting as $-m^{2}/H^{2}$). Let $I$ be a nonzero non-null ambient vector and $P = M(I)$ the grip of Definition \claimref{obj.mass.grip-mass-composite}. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $Q(P) = Q(I)\,\bigl(C_{2}(\Lambda^{2}\CS) - C_{2}(I^{\perp})\bigr)$;
\item $C_{2}(\Lambda^{2}\CS) = \tr(h^{2}) - \tfrac14(\tr h)^{2}$, for any number of constituents;
\item hence, for two conformal spinors, $I = I_{\Lambda}$ and $\Lambda \neq 0$, the de Sitter Casimir of the composite is
\[
C_{2}(I_{\Lambda}^{\perp}) = \tfrac14(\tr h)^{2} + C - \frac{6\,|B_{\Lambda}|^{2}}{\Lambda}.
\]
\end{enumerate}
Consequently (1) says that the grip $Q(P)$ equals the de Sitter mass $-Q(I)\,C_{2}(I^{\perp})$ plus $Q(I)\,C_{2}(\Lambda^{2}\CS)$, and the two coincide only where the conformal Casimir vanishes.

mass.exact-join.formal:
Let $I_{\Lambda} = n_{\infty} + \lambda n_{0}$ with $\lambda = \Lambda/6$ be the frozen point of Lemma \claimref{mass.de-sitter-scale-frozen-point}, and $Z^{1}, Z^{2} \in \CS$ two conformal spinors (twistors).
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(The deformed bracket.)} The pairing of the composite against the frozen point is
\begin{equation}
B_{\Lambda} := \ip{I_{\Lambda}}{Z^{1}\wedge Z^{2}} = \br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}} + \frac{\Lambda}{6}\,[\omega^{1}\omega^{2}],
\end{equation}
and at $\Lambda = 0$ it is the classical contraction of the composite with the point at infinity $n_{\infty}$, read as a bivector of $\CS$ (Penrose's infinity twistor).
\item \emph{(The mass.)} The grip $P = M(I_{\Lambda})$ has squared length
\begin{equation}
m^{2} = Q(P) = 2\,|B_{\Lambda}|^{2} + Q(I_{\Lambda})\, C = 2\,|B_{\Lambda}|^{2} + \frac{\Lambda}{3}\, C .
\end{equation}
\item \emph{(The flat shadow.)} At $\Lambda = 0$, $m^{2} = 2|\ip{I}{Z^{1}\wedge Z^{2}}|^{2} = 2|\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}|^{2}$: Proposition \claimref{mass.mass-symplectic-area-two-twistor-formula}, read as the squared pairing of the composite against the frozen point.
\item \emph{(The point-supported composite.)} If both spinors lie on the line $L_{x}$ of the point $x$, the composite bivector is $\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}X(x)$ and $B_{\Lambda} = \sigma(x)\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}} = \bigl(1 - \tfrac{\Lambda Q(x)}{12}\bigr)\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}$: the flat bracket read in the de Sitter scale.
\item \emph{(The massless locus.)} For $\Lambda > 0$, $m^{2} \ge 0$, and $m^{2} = 0$ if and only if $B_{\Lambda} = 0$ and $h$ is a multiple of the identity; that multiple is zero, so the condition is $B_{\Lambda} = 0$ and $h = 0$ (\claimref{synthesis.many-twistor-join}(v)). The doubled composite $Z^{2} = Z^{1}$ has $B_{\Lambda} = 0$ and $m^{2} = \tfrac{\Lambda}{3}\,(\bar{Z}\cdot Z)^{2}$, so it is massless only when its constituent is null; and there are massless composites that are not doubled, for instance $Z^{1} = e_{1} + e_{4}$, $Z^{2} = \alpha e_{1} + \beta e_{4}$ with $\alpha \neq \beta$.
\end{enumerate}

mass.mass-symplectic-area-two-twistor-fo.formal:
On the real slice, where $\varepsilon^{A'B'}$ is the conjugate of $\varepsilon^{AB}$ and $\bar\pi$ the conjugate of $\pi$ (the conjugation reverses the complex unit $I_{6}$): a single conformal spinor (twistor) carries null momentum $p_{AA'} = \pi_{A}\bar\pi_{A'}$ and is massless, and a system of two conformal spinors with momentum
\[
p_{AA'} = \pi^{1}_{A}\bar\pi^{1}_{A'} + \pi^{2}_{A}\bar\pi^{2}_{A'}
\]
has
\begin{equation}
m^{2} = p^{2} = 2\,\bigl|\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}\bigr|^{2},
\end{equation}
the squared symplectic area between the two spinor parts. Aligned spinors (vanishing bracket) give a null composite, and mass grows with the misalignment.

proofs.main:
Contract with the spinor metric: $p^{2} = \varepsilon^{AB}\varepsilon^{A'B'} p_{AA'}\, p_{BB'}$. Expanding the product gives four terms. The two diagonal terms vanish by antisymmetry, $\varepsilon^{AB}\pi^{i}_{A}\pi^{i}_{B} = 0$; for a single conformal spinor this is already the whole computation, $p^{2} = 0$. The two cross terms are equal (exchange the roles of the two spinors, which relabels $A \leftrightarrow B$ and $A' \leftrightarrow B'$, two transpositions, and leaves both epsilons unchanged), and each is
\[
\varepsilon^{AB}\pi^{1}_{A}\pi^{2}_{B}\;\cdot\;\varepsilon^{A'B'}\bar\pi^{1}_{A'}\bar\pi^{2}_{B'}
= \br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}\cdot\overline{\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}}
= \bigl|\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}\bigr|^{2}
\]
on the real slice, where $\varepsilon^{A'B'}$ is the conjugate of $\varepsilon^{AB}$ under the conjugation reversing $I_{6}$ and the primed bracket is therefore the conjugate of the unprimed one. Hence $p^{2} = 2|\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}|^{2}$. The result is classical \cite{Hughston1979}; it is reproved here because the join needs every sign. The contraction identities are checked by the script on exact Gaussian-rational spinor instances.

mass.weight-composite.formal:
Let $\Lambda > 0$. Define the \emph{induced weight} of a composite of mass $m$ as the weight of a density with that mass under the dictionary, that is, a solution $w$ of $-w(w+3)H^{2} = m^{2}$. For the composite of two conformal spinors with $m^{2}$ given by \claimref{mass.exact-join}:
\begin{equation}
w = -\frac32 \pm I_4\nu, \qquad \nu^{2} = \frac{6\,|B_{\Lambda}|^{2}}{\Lambda} + C - \frac94,
\end{equation}
numbers being read in $\mathrm{span}\{1, I_4\}$, the weight being real exactly when $6|B_{\Lambda}|^{2}/\Lambda + C \le \tfrac94$, and otherwise on the principal series with $\nu = m/H - \tfrac98 H/m + O((H/m)^{3})$. The real part is $-\tfrac32$ for every composite heavier than $3H/2$; the mass lies in the imaginary part, and for $|B_{\Lambda}| \gg H\sqrt{C}$, which is the regime of every known particle when $C$ is of order one, $\mathrm{Im}\,w = \sqrt{2}\,|B_{\Lambda}|/H$ at leading order.

mass.weight-polynomial-two-series.formal:
In $n = 4$, with $\Delta := -w$ and $H^{2} = \Lambda/3 > 0$, the dictionary reads $m^{2} = \Delta(3-\Delta)H^{2}$, the de Sitter mass formula of Fact \claimref{mass.imported-unitary-representations-de-sitter-group}, whose two series are named below by that Fact; what is proved here is the algebra of $-w(w+3)$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $m = 0$ exactly for $w \in \{0, -3\}$, the shadow pair $w \leftrightarrow -3-w$ preserving $m^{2}$;
\item the conformally coupled scalar $w = 1 - n/2 = -1$ has $m^{2} = 2H^{2}$; in general dimension $w = 1 - n/2$ gives $m^{2} = \tfrac{n(n-2)}{4}Q(I)$;
\item for real $w$, $m^{2} = \bigl[\tfrac94 - (w + \tfrac32)^{2}\bigr]H^{2} \le \tfrac94 H^{2}$, with equality at $w = -\tfrac32$; $m^{2} \ge 0$ exactly for $w \in [-3, 0]$, where $m$ runs over $[0, 3H/2]$, the complementary series $0 < m < 3H/2$ together with its two endpoints (the massless pair and the $\nu = 0$ member of the principal series); a real weight outside $[-3,0]$ gives $m^{2} < 0$, outside both series of Fact \claimref{mass.imported-unitary-representations-de-sitter-group} that this paper uses;
\item for $m > 3H/2$ the weight is complex,
\begin{equation}
w = -\frac32 \pm I_4\nu, \qquad \nu = \sqrt{\frac{m^{2}}{H^{2}} - \frac94}, \qquad \nu = \frac{m}{H} - \frac98\,\frac{H}{m} + O\!\Bigl(\frac{H^{3}}{m^{3}}\Bigr),
\end{equation}
the principal series (numbers are read in $\mathrm{span}\{1, I_4\}$): the real part is universal and the mass lies in the imaginary part.
\end{enumerate}

standard-model.grip-mass-two-rung-module.formal:
At $\Lambda = 0$, with $I = n_{\infty}$, let $\Psi$ be a state of one chirality half of the two-rung module, so that every slot component $\psi_{a}$ lies in $\CS$ or in $\CSm{}$, the conformal spinor space or its dual (twistor space or its dual). Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $P(\Psi) = \sum_{a} p_{a}$, a sum of $\sum_{j}\binom{2}{j}$ null, future-directed momenta, one per slot component, each the momentum of a single conformal spinor or single dual conformal spinor;
\item[(ii)] a state in a slot-singlet sector, $j = 0$ or $j = 2$, has exactly one slot component and $m^{2}(\Psi) = 0$: the grip mass vanishes identically on the sectors $(0,0), (0,2), (2,0), (2,2)$, which under Hypothesis (S) are the species $\nu^{c}, u^{c}, e^{c}, d^{c}$ of the generation, and on their conjugates;
\item[(iii)] a state in the slot-doublet sector has two slot components with spinor parts $\pi^{1}, \pi^{2}$ and $m^{2}(\Psi) = 2|\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}|^{2}$, the two-spinor area formula, vanishing exactly when the components' spinor parts are proportional; these are the sectors $(1,1), (1,3)$, under Hypothesis (S) the species $Q$ and $L$ of the generation;
\item[(iv)] for the $n$-rung module the same holds with $\binom{n}{j}$ components, and $m^{2} = 2\sum_{a < b}|\br{\pi^{a}\pi^{b}}|^{2}$ over the components of a state in rung sector $j$; in particular the slot-singlet sectors $j = 0$ and $j = n$ are massless at every $n$.
\end{enumerate}

standard-model.moment-map-grip-mass-are-.formal:
Let $I \in V$ be any ambient vector of the ground completion, in particular the frozen point at any $\Lambda$, and let the module carry a product form. Then for every state $\Psi$ of the two-rung module:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the moment $M_{U \wedge I}(\Psi)$, the grip $P(\Psi)$ and $m^{2}(\Psi)$ are invariant under the rung mixing $U(2)$ and are computed sector by sector: writing $\Psi = \Psi_{0} + \Psi_{1} + \Psi_{2}$ by rung number, $M_{U \wedge I}(\Psi) = \sum_{j} M_{U \wedge I}(\Psi_{j})$ and $P(\Psi) = \sum_{j} P(\Psi_{j})$, with no cross-sector terms;
\item[(ii)] within a sector, the moment is the sum over slot components: for $\Psi_{j} = \sum_{a} e_{a} \otimes \psi_{a}$, $M_{U \wedge I}(\Psi_{j}) = \sum_{a} \ip{\psi_{a}}{A_{W}(U \wedge I)\psi_{a}}_{W}$ and $P(\Psi_{j}) = \sum_{a} P_{W}(\psi_{a})$, where $A_{W}$ and $P_{W}$ are the generator and the grip of Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-grip-area-casimir-form} on $\Lambda^{\bullet}W$;
\item[(iii)] the grip of a state depends on its slot components only through the multiset $\{\psi_{a}\}$: the same components placed at any slot states, or in any slot sector of the same size, give the same grip and the same mass.
\end{enumerate}

standard-model.transported-frozen-point-.formal:
Let the tower be built with its frames fixed, so that the rung generators are constants (Fact \claimref{standard-model.imported-tower-commuting-copies-fock-module}) and the transported ambient bundle is $V \oplus \R^{2,2}$ with the rung plane a trivial flat summand. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the parallel sections of the transported ambient bundle are $I \oplus c$ with $I$ parallel in $V$ and $c \in \R^{2,2}$ constant; the mass paper's frozen point is the parallel scale tractor of the ground conformal structure, so it has $c = 0$ by definition, and a nonzero $c$ is a datum the conformal structure does not fix;
\item[(ii)] under $S(U(2) \times U(3))$, $\R^{2,2} \otimes \C = R_{2} \oplus \overline{R}_{2} = (2,1)_{1/2} \oplus (2,1)_{-1/2}$: a rung component is a weak doublet of hypercharge $\pm\tfrac12$, the quantum numbers of the Higgs doublet; and a nonzero real $c = \alpha\cdot v + \beta\cdot w$ breaks the slot mixing $U(2)$ to a $U(1)$ when $\beta$ is a multiple of $\bar\alpha$ (for instance $c$ along one rung, as for the frozen point's lift), and breaks it completely otherwise;
\item[(iii)] the grip on $I \oplus c$ is $P = M(I) + M(c)$, and the generators $A(U \wedge c)$, $U \in V$, are mixed bivectors, a vector of $V$ times a vector of the rung plane, acting as $x_{W} \otimes y_{R}$: they change the rung number and the pair number by one each and have no matrix element within any sector. Hence pure-sector states keep the masses of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.grip-mass-two-rung-module} at first order in $c$, and the mass-squared acquires cross terms only between slot-doublet and slot-singlet sectors of one chirality: under Hypothesis (S), on the generation, between $Q$ and each of $\nu^{c}, u^{c}, e^{c}, d^{c}$, and between $L$ and $u^{c}, d^{c}$, never between $L$ and $e^{c}$ or $\nu^{c}$. This is not the pattern of the Yukawa couplings, which pair $L$ with $e^{c}$ and $\nu^{c}$; but the grip is a sesquilinear moment, and a mass term between two Weyl species is a bilinear, the invariant pairing of two sixteens through a vector, and through that pairing the same rung component joins exactly the Yukawa pairs, a computation the section “The vector of the module, and the Yukawa pattern” carries out.
\end{enumerate}
So a rung component is exactly what would make the frozen point sensitive to the slots, it has the Higgs doublet's quantum numbers, it is not fixed by anything derived in the series, and it is a Higgs in the sense that matters, the mass-term sense, though not in the grip's: the scalar sector of Proposition \claimref{standard-model.reduction} has, in the frozen point completed, a candidate the section “The vector of the module, and the Yukawa pattern” and the section “The frozen point, transported” take up.

synthesis.operator-join-casimir-shifts.formal:
Quantise a single conformal spinor ($N = 1$) with the palatial ordering of Proposition \claimref{synthesis.operator-join-ordering-constants}: $\hat{m}^{2} = Q(\hat{P})$ is the operator grip on the frozen point $I_{\Lambda}$ of Lemma \claimref{mass.de-sitter-scale-frozen-point}, $\hat{h}$ the palatial helicity generator (the symmetrised $\bar{Z}\cdot Z$), $\hat{C}_{\mathrm{conf}}$ the conformal Casimir $C_{2}(\Lambda^{2}\CS) = \tr(h^{2}) - \tfrac14(\tr h)^{2}$ of Proposition \claimref{mass.casimir-form-join} with its products Jordan-symmetrised, and define the operator de Sitter Casimir by the split of that proposition's (1), $\hat{C}_{\mathrm{dS}} := \hat{C}_{\mathrm{conf}} - \hat{m}^{2}/Q(I_{\Lambda})$, with $Q(I_{\Lambda}) = \Lambda/3 = H^{2}$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] in the oscillator algebra, $\hat{m}^{2} = \tfrac{\Lambda}{12}(\hat{h}^{2} - 4\hbar^{2})$, $\hat{C}_{\mathrm{conf}} = \tfrac34(\hat{h}^{2} - 4\hbar^{2})$ and $\hat{C}_{\mathrm{dS}} = \tfrac12(\hat{h}^{2} - 4\hbar^{2})$, whose classical values are $\tfrac{\Lambda}{12}h^{2}$, $\tfrac34 h^{2}$ and $\tfrac12 h^{2}$;
\item[(ii)] on functions of homogeneity $n$, where $\hat{h} = -(n + 2)\hbar = 2s\hbar$ (Fact \claimref{mass.imported-palatial-quadratics-homogeneity-charge}), $\hat{m}^{2} = H^{2}(s^{2} - 1)\hbar^{2}$, $\hat{C}_{\mathrm{conf}} = 3(s^{2} - 1)\hbar^{2}$ and $\hat{C}_{\mathrm{dS}} = 2(s^{2} - 1)\hbar^{2}$: in all three the ordering replaces $s^{2}$ by $s^{2} - 1$;
\item[(iii)] on the scalar module, $n = -2$, $s = 0$, the Penrose transform's conformally invariant scalar (Fact \claimref{field-theory.imported-standard-field-theory}), $\hat{C}_{\mathrm{dS}} = -2\hbar^{2}$. If the moment normalisation of the de Sitter Casimir is the scalar-module one of Fact \claimref{mass.imported-unitary-representations-de-sitter-group} (acting as $-m^{2}/H^{2}$), this is $m^{2} = 2H^{2}\hbar^{2}$, $\Delta \in \{1, 2\}$, the conformally coupled value $-w(w+3)H^{2}$ at $w = -1$ of Theorem \claimref{mass.mass-weight-dictionary} and Corollary \claimref{mass.weight-polynomial-two-series}(2), whereas the grip is $\hat{m}^{2} = -H^{2}\hbar^{2}$ and the classical de Sitter Casimir gives $0$.
\end{enumerate}
For any number of constituents the de Sitter reading $-H^{2}\hat{C}_{\mathrm{dS}}$ and the grip differ by $H^{2}\hat{C}_{\mathrm{conf}}$; under the same normalisation hypothesis, the grip is the de Sitter mass only on states with $\hat{C}_{\mathrm{conf}} = 0$, which for one spinor means $|s| = 1$.

synthesis.operator-join-seed-has-no-diag.formal:
Let $\hat{B} = \hat{b} + \lambda\hat{\Omega}$, $\lambda = \Lambda/6$, with $\hat{b} = \br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}$ and $\hat{\Omega} = [\omega^{1}\omega^{2}]$ polynomials in the palatial oscillators $Z^{\alpha}_{i}$ of two constituents, $\hat{B}^{\dagger}$, $\hat{b}^{\dagger}$, $\hat{\Omega}^{\dagger}$ their conjugates (polynomials in the $\bar{Z}_{i}$), $\hat{h}_{il}$ the symmetrised Gram operators and $\hat{m}^{2} = \hat{B}^{\dagger}\hat{B} + \hat{B}\hat{B}^{\dagger} + \tfrac{\Lambda}{3}\hat{C} - \tfrac{2\Lambda}{3}\hbar^{2}$ the operator join of Proposition \claimref{synthesis.operator-join-ordering-constants}(3). Let $\hat{S} = \tfrac{\Lambda}{3}(\bar{A}\hat{b} + A\hat{b}^{\dagger})$ be the source term of Proposition \claimref{families.source-term-linear-tilt-mass-functional}, the part of $2|B_{\Lambda}|^{2}$ linear in $b$ with the vacuum $\omega$-area a constant $A$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(The join is internally invariant.)} $[\hat{h}_{il}, \hat{m}^{2}] = 0$ for $i, l \in \{1, 2\}$; in particular $\hat{m}^{2}$ commutes with each constituent's helicity generator $\hat{h}_{ii}$, which acts on functions of homogeneity $n_{i}$ in $Z_{i}$ as $-(n_{i} + 2)\hbar$.
\item \emph{(The bracket and the area carry the determinant charge.)} $[\hat{h}_{ii}, \hat{X}] = -\hbar\hat{X}$ and $[\hat{h}_{ii}, \hat{X}^{\dagger}] = \hbar\hat{X}^{\dagger}$ for $\hat{X} \in \{\hat{b}, \hat{\Omega}\}$ and $i = 1, 2$, and $[\hat{h}_{12}, \hat{X}] = [\hat{h}_{21}, \hat{X}] = 0$. So $\hat{S}$ maps the joint eigenspace of $(\hat{h}_{11}, \hat{h}_{22})$ with eigenvalues $(t_{1}, t_{2})$ into the sum of those with $(t_{1} \mp \hbar, t_{2} \mp \hbar)$, and has no block on the diagonal.
\item \emph{(No seed on a state of definite helicities.)} For any Hermitian form for which $\hat{h}_{11}$ and $\hat{h}_{22}$ are self-adjoint, $\langle\psi, \hat{S}\psi\rangle = 0$, $\langle\psi, \hat{b}\psi\rangle = 0$ and $\langle\psi, \hat{\Omega}\psi\rangle = 0$ for every $\psi$ of definite constituent helicities. Hence on every module on which $\hat{h}_{11}$ and $\hat{h}_{22}$ act as scalars, in particular every product of irreducible positive-energy helicity modules, the source has zero expectation in every state, and in every eigenstate of $\hat{m}^{2}$ there, massless or not. A nonzero expectation of $\hat{S}$, or of the vacuum $\omega$-area it presupposes, requires a state with components in two joint eigenspaces whose eigenvalues differ by $\hbar(1,1)$ (constituent helicities each differing by $\tfrac12$), which is not invariant under the determinant $U(1)$ of the internal $U(2)$, a symmetry of the join. Since $\hat{m}^{2}$ preserves each joint eigenspace, a massless state with nonzero seed exists only if $\hat{m}^{2}$ has zero modes in two such neighbouring eigenspaces; whether it does is not decided here.
\end{enumerate}
